us  and and then um if we also  knew about the patient uh that had  unusual clinical course  and that disease did not respond what  respond well to treatment that the  patient relapse and then made that made  it even more important  to understand what was genetics um of  that particular leukemia  in essence there are many genetic  abnormalities that are known to be  associated with high risk and aggressive  disease so those are our criteria but  then there was also practical criteria  and whether we really had the material  left and  and to do optical genome mapping um  because these were all  retrospective studies and it was all  based on cases that we tested previously  in the lab  and and sometimes we do have some  residual material left that sometimes  we don't so and that was there was that  practical limitation  wonderful and so the second part to this  audience question is  would you see this technology in a  routine diagnostic lab  along with ngs i think it has  really good potential to be to be used  um  and and to really be advantageous um  in clinical testing um there are some  laboratories that are very  um quickly very actively working on  on validating this work for clinical use  and because in the research setting it  has shown um  advantages in detection detecting  structural variants  it has it has many things that are  attractive when you look at a um  some a test being used in the clinical  setting  and and um  i know that um some labs are  already working on and validating it for  clinical use  and there was recently a very nice  symposium  um organized by nano with and speakers  from all over the world  and sharing their experience with  optical genome mapping and several  laboratories described  moving this towards clinical validation  and because as i mentioned in my talk  and it detects a very well structural  variance both  copy number changes and balance  structural rearrangements and which is  what you can do by karyotype um but  and it detects that with better  precision you know at higher resolution  and so and it it potentially could be  a very good alternative for to karyotype  analysis and fish testing  and specifically we we um  are interested in hematologic  malignancies so in that area  and and um we have  now our own hands-on you know firsthand  of our experience on our own cases  showing  and that it detects um abnormalities  that we missed by other  technologies so musa i certainly see  potential  and i know of laboratories that are um  already validating it for clinical use  great thank you so much so um back to  another two-part question for you dr  raka  when validating fusions using rna-seq  was the sequence mapping done against a  reference genome or  a reference transcriptome and why did  you choose that reference type  um so it was um it was a  mapped against reference transcriptome  and i wish i could give you  a lot of technical details um  but but i don't because um i'm not  bioinformatician by training  we have an uh bioinformatics team at  chla  and that was um helping us to develop  and and  um implement this test and so  mapping is against the transcriptome but  unfortunately i don't  really get um we have a lot of technical  details  that you can share today i'm certainly i  will certainly be happy to  provide those details in in the future  through  email or other methods  great now do you believe um  uh i'm so sorry here's our next question  so  what cma testing platform are you using  our laboratory uses um a  cytoskin hd arrays from term of fisher  previously developed by fmetrix so  that's what we use  whenever we have fresh material  and both for our constitutional cases  and for our oncology cases  when we get fresh material which is most  often the case with leukemias  and and then for some subset of our  solid tumors um but we did validate  um anka scan platform as well um for  ffbe  samples you know we have that valid in  the web as well and use that  when we get fmp  so we do have time for a couple more  questions but i do want to remind our  audience  any questions that we aren't able to get  to and those that come in during the on  demand period  will be addressed by dr rocco via the uh  email address that you provided at  registration  so dr raka do you think that there is a  gender bias  for example male predominance for  leukemia  driven by the mib gata one fusion and  if yes what is the possible mechanism  yeah thank you for that question i  really thought that that case with  me gotta one fusion was quite  fascinating  um and and i since all  other previous cases were indeed a male  um that might indicate that there could  be potentially gender bias  and interestingly it has been described  for other genes that  map on x chromosome and for other um  oncogene and tumor suppressor genes that  map um  an x chromosome and that there seems to  be  uh if when they are key drivers that  they that they are more commonly seen in  male patients  and so although and functionally male  females also have just one active copy  of those genes  and there still seems to be in other  cases as well  um some male predominance when the gene  is is mapped on  x and but what exactly would be the  mechanism is a very i think interesting  uh research question and whether there  is  um some as i mentioned earlier some type  of compensatory  reactivation of the inactive copy in  females and that makes those  um those leukemias those genes  makes it more difficult for these genes  to um to be  fully oncogenic and realize their  oncogenic potential  and so i think that and that that is is  very very interesting uh research  question um that  we we might look into or i i'm hoping  that some other groups  uh will be interested in but to answer  the question  directly i think that there might be  some male bias um based on  on the fact that other reported cases  were all in male patients  wonderful so it looks like we have time  for  one more question so talk to raka first  off thank you for the fantastic webinar  would you like to comment on the impact  of the percentage of  abnormal clone on the utilization of ogm  um yeah that's always a big concern in  cancer testing and we want to be able to  detect abnormalities that are present  at a low level and and i know that there  are studies out there that looked into  sensitivity of optical genome mapping  for  abnormalities present at low level so it  depends a little bit um similar to ngs  depends a little bit on and how um  if i could put it that way depth of  coverage or how many reads  you're going to analyze but i know that  they have shown in previous studies that  um it can go down to five percent  sensitivity so it can detect  abnormalities that are present  as low as five percent and and then if  you really push it  um and and do a very um high coverage  maybe even lower than that  wonderful thank you again dr raka for  your time today and your important  research  we would also like to thank lavers and  our sponsor bionanogenomics for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us oh  excuse me i've re-said that um and  they're interesting questions uh  questions that we did not have time for  today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by our speaker  via the contact information  that you provided at the time of  registration this webcast can be viewed  on demand  labyrints will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  that's all for now thank you for joining  us stay healthy  stay safe and until next time bye-bye  everyone
 

